Subject: virtual-fs: lock the memory filesystem once per operation
Last-Update: 2026-09-30

The in-memory filesystem looked a name up under its read lock, released
it, and made the change under the write lock. remove_file, remove_dir,
rename and an open file's unlink then removed a position that another
thread's removal could have made stale: Vec::remove panicked and
poisoned the lock, after which every operation failed. open,
create_dir, create_symlink and the insert helpers created an entry that
another thread could have created meanwhile, so an exclusive create or
a mkdir could succeed twice and leave two entries of one name, and open
could reach a file whose inode had been freed and reused. Each now looks
up and changes under one write lock, released before a path is handed
on to another mounted filesystem, and create_dir refuses a name taken
by a file as well as by a directory.

The other way round, a handle took the write lock to read, to seek and
to flush, though none of them changes the filesystem: a read and a seek
move the handle's own cursor, and every memory-backed node's flush is a
no-op. Readers of different files serialised on one lock; they now take
the read lock, and the no-op flush methods are gone.

The remove_file test held a read guard while calling remove_file, which
now waits for the write lock, so it releases the guard first. New tests
race exclusive creates and directory creation across threads.

